  "id": "Compound::DB00091 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "Cyclosporine is implicated in the regulation of multiple diseases through its interaction with Cyp2c9, a key enzyme in the cytochrome P450 family. The evidence suggests that Cyclosporine may inhibit Cyp2c9 through a mechanism involving the binding of carboxamide groups, as indicated by the lowest common ancestor (LCA) of Cyclosporine and its metabolites, which are classified as carboxamide compounds. This interaction is supported by the observed binding of Leflunomide, a drug associated with Pustular Psoriasis, to Cyp2c9, implying a potential pathway where Cyclosporine could modulate Cyp2c9 activity. Similarly, the interaction with Quinine and Ritonavir, both of which bind to Cyp2c9, further reinforces the possibility of Cyclosporine influencing Cyp2c9 function. The consistent presence of Cyp2c9 as a target across multiple disease pathways, coupled with the shared carboxamide class, suggests a mechanistic link that is biologically plausible. The evidence supports a role for Cyclosporine in modulating Cyp2c9 activity, potentially through carboxamide-mediated inhibition, which could contribute to the pathophysiology of the diseases associated with these interactions.",
